SM(515,{"n":"Romans 8 Conclusion","a":"Fr Stephen De Young Archives/Romans 8 Conclusion.mp3","d":2657.7,"s":[[0.16,"Come and study Holy Scriptures with us as Father Stephen DeYoung teaches verse by verse on the podcast The Whole Counsel of God."],[8.08,"Father Stephen is a former Protestant pastor and Bible teacher in the Reformed Church, but now an Orthodox priest serving at Archangel Gabriel Orthodox Church in Lafayette, Louisiana."],[21.68,"Okay, so go ahead and get started, and in just a moment when we get started, we're picking up, as I mentioned in Romans 8, verse 28, to quickly kind of get us caught up, and it's uh it's kind of important that we can get caught up on the earlier part of chapter 8, because as we get into here the second half of chapter 8, and then into chapter 9 and chapter 10 and chapter 11, as I mentioned before we started recording, these are passages about which Calvinists get very excited."],[51.44,"And part of uh the reason they get excited is that they have an interpretation of these passages that's sort of the basis for their whole thing."],[59.84,"They will take you, it's called the Romans' Road in Calvinist circles"],[61.24,"And it's not just these passages."],[67.0,"They will sort of hop skip through the book of Romans."],[69.8,"You generally do one through three, and then the first half of chapter five, and then chapters eight through eleven."],[77.88,"And if you read those chunks along a certain trajectory, you can kind of get Calvinism out of them."],[86.76,"Now, obvious problem there already is that there are certain parts we're skipping and ignoring and not focusing on, which means we're taking the passages we are reading sort of out of context."],[100.12,"And so that's why it's important that we set this second half of chapter eight here in terms of the first half of chapter eight."],[109.46,"The lead in, the themes that St."],[111.86,"Paul has already been talking about before he gets to what he's going to talk about here in the second half, so that we can understand sort of the flow, the flow of his overall argument and not take anything out of context."],[127.14,"So St. Paul began chapter eight by making a contrast between people walking according to the flesh and walking according to the spirit."],[137.54,"And we talked about how flesh and body, even though we would use those interchangeably, refer to two different things in St."],[145.14,"Paul. And when St."],[146.5,"Paul talks about our body, he's talking about our physical body."],[150.46,"And that is something which, as he said earlier in chapter 8, is going to be redeemed when Christ returns."],[157.5,"He's going to talk about and be very clear about, especially when we get to 1 Corinthians and 1 Corinthians chapter 15, the resurrection and transformation and transfiguration of our of our bodies, our physical bodies."],[169.82,"So for Saint Paul, the physical body, physicality, creation as such, the material world is not bad, is not evil, is not wicked."],[179.98,"Contra a lot of Greek thought, at the at this time."],[184.48,"The flesh, on the other hand, is that part of us, that part of our current body, which is prone to sin."],[195.08,"Is our, who uses the phrase mortal flesh, or our mortal body sometimes to refer to the flesh."],[201.96,"It's that part of us that's subject to sin and death."],[205.4,"And it's that part of our bodies that was received after the expulsion from paradise."],[213.48,"Where Christ, we're told, uh, clothes them with garments of skin."],[219.4,"Uh, that's not understood by the fathers to be referring to God made them a neat leather outfit."],[225.37,"But that now, just as our bodies are going to be transformed in the resurrection, as we saw Christ's body was transformed when he rose from the dead, in the same way, uh, there was a negative transformation after the expulsion from paradise, uh, where their bodies became first of all mortal, subject to death, therefore subject to sin, to wandering astray."],[250.07,"And so St."],[251.11,"Paul has set up in Romans 8 that there are two ways in which we can choose to walk, two ways in which we can live our lives."],[258.71,"We can follow our flesh, we can follow that sinful part of ourselves that desires things in this world, where we live our life just trying to gratify it, just trying to give it what it wants."],[269.13,"Food and drink and sex and wealth and all the things that our flesh desires, or we could follow after the Spirit, meaning the Holy Spirit, who, because uh Christ has purified us now, has come to dwell within us, and so we could follow the Spirit, we could seek the things of the Spirit, we could seek to try to gratify God through His Spirit and live our life following the Spirit's leading."],[297.01,"After setting up that, and and in within that comparison is the way that follows the flesh leads to death, because it is chasing after the things of this world, and this world is passing away."],[308.77,"Whereas the way of following the spirit leads to life, meaning eternal life."],[313.43,"Life in the world to come."],[315.27,"Paul then moved to talking about the sufferings of this life"],[315.55,"And so St."],[320.79,"Because in this life we suffer, but he talks about how the spirit who dwells within us is sort of a down payment and a deposit on the world to come."],[327.19,"in. He's the begning of our salvation"],[333.71,"And so he gives us hope in the midst of all of the suffering that we experience in this life, all the troubles we feel in this life, as sort of proof and a down payment of what the glory is that awaits us at the end."],[350.69,"As we draw close to God."],[352.53,"And if we follow the Spirit, we'll progress towards that glorious end."],[356.93,"And St. Paul has said that he thinks that all the troubles of this life, and he experienced a whole bunch of them, being shipwrecked and stoned several times and beaten, and they rejected all these things."],[367.81,"That it isn't even worth comparing to the glory that that. will be ours in the world to come"],[374.25,"And he's talked about that in terms of our adoptions, adoption as sons, which isn't just a metaphor of sort of love, but remember, God's family is a royal family."],[384.97,"The sons of God in the Old Testament are not just angels, but they're sort of the highest rank of angels who form God's council around him."],[393.77,"And so it's adoption into this royal family and receiving the spiritual riches and that role in the world to come of helping God to govern his creation as Christ gives us the privilege of sharing in his in his rule over creation."],[412.33,"That's how St."],[413.69,"Paul, in the earlier part of chapter 8, defines this glorious destiny that we have ahead of us."],[417.13,"And so whatever we have to suffer in this life doesn't compare to that"],[424.21,"If we ever start to doubt because of the suffering of this life, that then we have the spirit as a signifier and a down payment dwelling within us to show us that that is real."],[437.49,"So now it's within the context of that discussion that St."],[442.05,"Paul is going to continue here in Romans 8:28."],[447.25,"We kind of have to have that lead in in mind so that we don't misunderstand what he's about to say as he starts talking about things like predestination and what that means."],[459.13,"So unless we have any leftover questions or comments or allegations or business proposals or anything else."],[470.33,"That'd be a quick question."],[471.37,"Yeah. Kind of irrelevant."],[472.81,"But with one of the things you were saying with Genesis it talked about uh man being clothed in skin."],[479.78,"Um does uh does it differ between uh the Hebrew or the Greek translation of it uh on uh the interpretation of how that's interpreted no it's b it basically says garments of skin and so the reason why people have taken sometimes take of the other interpretation is that comes right after God kills some animals in front of Adam and Eve."],[501.22,"So they think, oh well, God killed some animals to make them clothes."],[505.26,"Which doesn't make a lot of sense in context."],[508.06,"Um what the fathers say is that the reason God killed those animals was to show them what death was."],[515.9,"You know, because they've been told they were going to die, and now they're telling it you're going to die."],[520.14,"And so he shows them what what death is."],[524.62,"And then they're they're clothed with the body that is like the body of those animals in that it's now mortal, it's subject to death."],[534.38,"Which the animals were, but they were not uh before the expulsion from paradise."],[541.42,"So the animals before the fall were mortal."],[546.46,"Yes. Okay. Yeah."],[549.18,"Never asked that question to myself before."],[555.38,"Because uh remember that the immortality would come with eating from the tree of life for human beings."],[563.22,"So Romans 8, 28."],[566.82,"And we know that all things work together for good to those who love God, to those who are the called according to his purpose."],[576.1,"I'll just go ahead and read the next verse."],[579.06,"Or a couple of verses."],[580.5,"For whom he foreknew he also predestined to be conformed to the image of his son, that he might be the firstborn among many brethren."],[587.94,"Moreover, whom he predestined, these he also called, whom he called, these he also justified, and whom he justified, these he also glorified."],[597.5,"Okay. So I can get Calvinism out of that."],[602.22,"Yes, this is crucially important to get, especially if you take it out of out of context."],[607.52,"And just take it by itself."],[609.04,"So the first verse is non controversial, I would mostly."],[614.06,"That all things work together for good for those who love God."],[618.38,"And at least that much of it."],[621.02,"And remember, this is in the context of St."],[622.86,"Paul having just talked about suffering."],[625.26,"So what he's saying here is that even those things we suffer, even those things in this life that are negative, that are unpleasant, that are painful, ultimately God uses those to work for our good."],[639.44,"It's not just that we have to endure them, but that through them we can actually develop into more mature spiritual people."],[649.96,"Now notice, remember, I've I've commented before, if you're reading this in the Orthodox Study Bible, that words that are in italics are not there in the original."],[661.72,"So that's important here, too."],[664.84,"Because those are words that were added by someone who's doing an interpretation."],[670.44,"So we always have to be a little careful with those."],[673.0,"So those who love God are here identified as those who are the ones called."],[681.32,"What it literally says, you notice they've inserted the word his according to his purpose, where what it actually says is who are called according to a purpose."],[692.08,"The word his is not there."],[693.84,"So do you already see how that shifts the meaning?"],[696.96,"Called according to his purpose means God has chosen to call some people for his reasons."],[703.1,"Whereas called for a purpose means it's talking about people who God has called to something."],[712.03,"You're called to do something."],[713.71,"So this isn't necessarily talking about salvation."],[717.15,"First of all."],[718.32,"You're called for a purpose."],[719.92,"You're called to do something."],[722.16,"Then it says, for those those whom he foreknew, he also predestined."],[727.76,"Now notice that to be."],[731.94,"Is added. He predestined to be conformed to the image of his son, that he might be the firstborn among many brethren."],[740.74,"And then he says, Moreover, whom he predestined, these he also called, and whom he called he also justified, and whom he justified, these he also glorified."],[749.7,"So the the first problem with the Calvinist interpretation of this is that the Calvinist understanding of this verse is that this is what they call the Ordo Salutis."],[759.06,"This is the order of salvation."],[760.9,"These things happen in sequence."],[763.98,"Okay, so they say, so first God foreknows people, and then because he foreknows them, he predestines them to be conform to the image of his son."],[776.06,"And then once he's predestined them, then he calls them, and then after calling them, he justifies them, and then after justifying them, he ultimately glorifies them."],[785.26,"What's the problem with that?"],[787.18,"Well, all of these words are in the exact same past tense."],[791.02,"So there's nothing here to indicate that these are things that take place in a certain order."],[797.02,"No, I didn't read it that way."],[799.18,"In fact, what St."],[800.62,"Paul is saying is that God doesn't just do one of these things."],[807.34,"You're not at stage two or something like that."],[810.08,"It's all or nothing."],[811.9,"It's all or nothing."],[814.54,"Now, interestingly, if you if you look in the church fathers about this verse, obviously Calvin hadn't lived yet."],[821.78,"And most of them had not yet read Augustine, even, who did something somewhat similar with this verse."],[828.82,"And so what they consider to be the big problem is that Christ said repeatedly in the Gospels, many are called, but few are chosen."],[837.06,"And this says everyone whom he predestined, he also called, and everyone who he called, he also justified."],[843.14,"So they consider that to be the problem with this with this passage."],[847.7,"So what I want to do is dig a little deeper into the into the words."],[853.3,"So the first word is those whom God knew beforehand."],[858.26,"Now it doesn't say that he knew something about them beforehand."],[861.94,"It just says that he knew them"],[865.31,"In the context of God knowing people, what do we see?"],[870.03,"Well, for example, the parable of the Last Judgment, the parable of the sheep and the goats in St."],[876.51,"Matthew's gospel. The goats, the wicked."],[880.89,"Christ says, be gone from me, I never knew you."],[885.29,"I never knew you."],[887.37,"So that doesn't literally mean, well, where'd you come from?"],[892.34,"Because obviously, God in Christ created everyone who has ever lived."],[897.12,"So it's not he doesn't literally doesn't know who they are and where they came from."],[902.93,"So this knowledge is referring to the faithful and the unfaithful."],[908.34,"Because the basis on which Christ said, I never knew you, is that he was poor and they had now he was hungry and they hadn't fed him."],[916.02,"He was right."],[916.82,"And this is true in several other parables where that language is used."],[920.6,"I never knew you, departed the outer dark."],[923.06,"So those who are known by God, being known by God."],[927.94,"Other languages related to this in the Old Testament is having their names written in the book of life."],[932.66,"That book language is common not only in the Old Testament, but in other ancient Near Eastern and Second Temple Jewish literature."],[939.94,"And it's the idea that there is a record of everything that happens."],[943.62,"It's in Relations."],[944.42,"And everyone that lives."],[945.46,"Yeah, the books were opened at the last judgment."],[948.1,"There are these heavenly books in which everything that happens is written down, meaning that, and what that means is that God knows about it."],[955.68,"This isn't slipping past him, or getting past his mind."],[960.5,"Okay. So we're the group we're starting out with here that St."],[966.26,"Paul is talking about, is this group of people who are known by God."],[969.94,"So these are the faithful people."],[972.27,"These are the faithful people who he's talking about."],[975.05,"And God, he's already said has a purpose for these people."],[978.25,"But what is the significance of efore kne?"],[982.41,"I mean, to the Calvinists, this means that he knew before creation."],[986.17,"Well, we're going to get into we're going to get to that just a second when we talk about uh predestination."],[991.61,"So this is is prognosco, which is pro, which before, obviously."],[997.19,"Prologue. Proactive. And uh yinosko is is to know."],[1002.49,"Okay, so it's very literally just he knew beforehand."],[1005.53,"But now the second word is what happens to these people is that they are pro orizo."],[1011.75,"That's the word that's translated predestined."],[1015.19,"And this predestined verb is setting them up to be conformed to the likeness of his son, so he could be the firstborn among many brethren."],[1025.19,"So this is the language he used earlier in chapter eight about us becoming sons of God and heirs within the kingdom, becoming part of this royal family."],[1035.43,"So this is what he did"],[1035.88,"Becoming like Christ."],[1037.72,"Okay. Now the word pro orizzo literally means to set things in order."],[1045.64,"Orizzo means to set things in order."],[1048.04,"It's a verb that's used a lot of times for uh when a general or a troop commander is setting his troops in order for battle."],[1057.96,"It means to organize."],[1059.74,"To set in to set in place."],[1062.06,"So what St."],[1062.78,"Paul is saying here is God beforehand knew that there would be these faithful people."],[1071.02,"Not in a general sense, like, oh, there will be some faithful people, but he he knew of a because God is outside of time."],[1077.9,"He's not sort of following along and he gets surprised by things."],[1079.66,"He knew who his faithful people would be"],[1083.76,"And so, in advance, he set things in place, he organized things so that they would be able to experience this glorious destiny that St."],[1095.84,"Paul has been talking about."],[1097.92,"Well, what does that mean he set things in order?"],[1100.24,"Well, Christ had to become incarnate."],[1104.96,"He had to die on the cross, he had to rise again on the third day, he had to ascend into heaven, he had to pour out his Holy Spirit on all flesh."],[1112.8,"All of these things had to happen for you and I to find salvation."],[1115.84,"And they all happened a long time before we existed."],[1119.44,"But God knew us already, knew that we would exist."],[1123.68,"And so for us, this is why we could say, I could say Christ died for my sins"],[1130.66,"That he rose again for my salvation, not just somebody's, but mine."],[1134.74,"Because he already knew me at the time he was doing it."],[1140.02,"All of that had to happen first."],[1142.02,"All of that had to happen so that God could bring me to that, to that destiny."],[1149.06,"And so God has been at work, not just then."],[1152.98,"I mean, we could go all the way back to Genesis from the moment they were expelled from paradise, which he knew would happen."],[1159.43,"God has been working to bring us back, giving the law everything that happened in the Old Testament, everything that happens in the New Testament, everything that's happened, and then everything that's happened in my life up till now."],[1169.87,"God made it that possible so that salvation would be a reality for his faithful people."],[1175.87,"And he was under no obligation to do so."],[1178.91,"He didn't have to."],[1194.91,"So what's predestined here is the whole plan of salvation."],[1200.03,"Yeah. Foreordained is probably a better translation than predestined."],[1205.39,"Because it's not predestined has this fatalistic sound of destiny."],[1209.95,"You're born with this destiny, there's nothing you could do about it."],[1212.07,"Which the original word doesn't have."],[1213.83,"Foreordained is better because it means beforehand he ordered, which is more literally what the what the word means."],[1222.39,"That he set things in order so that this could happen."],[1225.91,"So that we could become like Christ, so that we could become fellow heirs with Christ."],[1231.83,"With Christ as the firstborn."],[1234.15,"And I'll reiterate it here because this is going to become important here and again in chapter nine."],[1239.51,"Uh, that remember the way inheritance worked at this time in history and, for a long time before, we sometimes think, well, the firstborn son inherited everything."],[1248.63,"Sort of. He inherited everything, but then it was his responsibility to distribute it to the other the other heirs."],[1256.47,"And so, for example, there's an incident in the Gospels where someone comes to Christ and says, Tell my brother to divide the inheritance with me, because apparently he's been holding on to all the money."],[1266.68,"And Christ basically tells him, Don't worry about the money, come follow me."],[1270.92,"You know, let your brother be."],[1272.86,"If that's what he wants, is the money."],[1274.94,"But so that's that's how it worked."],[1276.86,"So it's not that there's one person who received the whole inheritance and everybody else could go lump it."],[1282.24,"That's gonna be important when we get to Jacob and Esau in the next chapter."],[1286.16,"But it's also important here because this is how St."],[1288.48,"Paul is talking about Christ and us as fellow heirs."],[1291.92,"Christ is the firstborn, Christ inherits the whole creation, but then he distributes that, he gives a share to us as his adopted, as his adopted brot.hers"],[1304.36,"So that's that language of being formed to his likeness and then the firstborn among many many brethren."],[1310.68,"And the reason he uses brother, this sometimes gets muted out because our modern translations are trying to be very PC."],[1319.24,"And so they don't like to use the word sons."],[1324.52,"But the word sons is important because son is an heir, in this culture."],[1329.74,"So St. Paul is trying to make the point that women who believe become sons."],[1336.38,"They become heirs."],[1337.94,"They're not, they may be second class citizens on earth in the first century AD or third or fourth class."],[1345.28,"But in terms of the family of God, the royal family of God, they're not second tier, they're not lesser."],[1352.57,"They are sons and heirs just as much as the men are, in terms of the inheritance they receive in Christ."],[1361.21,"So then those for whom he set this."],[1366.43,"For whom he set this, uh, all these things, he also called, meaning what?"],[1371.79,"Meaning we heard the gospel."],[1375.15,"The gospel was proclaimed, it was put out there."],[1378.51,"Because again, if Christ had done all those things for us, but we never heard about it."],[1385.85,"It wouldn't, it wouldn't avail anything."],[1388.57,"Those whom he he called, also justified."],[1391.17,"We talked about justification back in chapter three."],[1395.57,"Made righteous. Come to share in God's righteousness, meaning that when the judgment happens, we're the sheep, not the goats."],[1406.31,"Brought us into that category."],[1408.39,"And whom he justified, these he also glorified."],[1411.51,"Now, this is all in the past tense because there have already been a whole lot of saints by the time Saint Paul is writing."],[1418.31,"And so this has all happened for people."],[1421.51,"He's saying, look at these people."],[1422.87,"Look at St."],[1423.59,"Elias. Look at any of the Old Testament prophets."],[1427.11,"God has done this."],[1428.97,"He has done this whole thing."],[1430.57,"So this was retroactive for the Old Testament saints."],[1434.66,"They're glorified together with us."],[1436.65,"And that's part of the whole harrowing of hell depiction."],[1440.94,"But so why is he saying this?"],[1444.06,"This is in the context again of why we should have hope."],[1447.34,"Why we should have hope."],[1448.78,"We're in this life and we're suffering."],[1452.2,"We've decided that we're going to follow Christ."],[1455.0,"We're going to embrace Jesus as the Messiah."],[1457.24,"We're going to try and walk by the Spirit."],[1459.08,"And our family has disowned us."],[1462.29,"We become an outcast in our city."],[1464.93,"We've lost our livelihood."],[1467.01,"You might be tempted to say, W,ell hey, maybe we're wrong about this."],[1471.51,"Because God's not doing a lot to take care of me if this is really the right thing that I'm supposed to do."],[1474.31,"And so St"],[1478.19,"Paul is saying to that person, look, long before you existed, long before you were born, long before you were conceived."],[1486.85,"It came into being, God knew who you were."],[1491.33,"And he's done all of these things."],[1494.95,"All these things in the Old Testament, and then Christ's incarnation, his ministry, his death, his resurrection, his ascension, the coming of the Holy Spirit."],[1503.43,"He's done all these things so that you could be adopted into his royal family."],[1508.31,"He made provision for you to hear the gospel."],[1511.03,"He's cleansed you and purified you of your sins."],[1514.23,"Having done all of that for you, that all being the case, do you really think? he's not going to bring this through to the end"],[1521.31,"Just because you're suffering some things now."],[1523.63,"This is what St."],[1524.51,"Paul is saying."],[1525.87,"He's not talking to them about whether or not their actions are involved in their salvation."],[1532.35,"He's made a pretty clear statement about that at the beginning of the chapter."],[1535.79,"That those who follow the flesh perish and those who follow the spirit come to life."],[1541.39,"He's already talked about that."],[1542.59,"That's not what he's talking about."],[1546.02,"That's not what he's talking about here."],[1548.02,"He's talking about people who are given to lose hope and fall into despair and telling them why they shouldn't."],[1556.26,"Why they shouldn't."],[1558.18,"And this is a pretty powerful reason why you shouldn't."],[1562.36,"That's interesting because the effect of Calvinism was often to discourage people."],[1570.44,"Uh and uh make them think to talk about your wickedness and your inability, just having to hope that God picked you for his team."],[1583.1,"Which he would do for no reason, and so there was nothing you could do to get yourself picked."],[1587.44,"And it already happened."],[1588.8,"So you're picked or not already, yeah."],[1592.48,"St. Paul goes on in verse 31."],[1594.32,"What then shall we say to these things?"],[1596.32,"If God is for us, who can be against us?"],[1600.06,"So again, this is in that context of suffering."],[1602.91,"God is doing all these things for us."],[1605.59,"So who is there out there who can stop it?"],[1610.15,"Stop him, who can prevent him from bringing it to completion?"],[1614.47,"What can they do to us?"],[1617.85,"In the face of that."],[1619.61,"If they kill us, if they kill you, well then now I will go to eternal life."],[1625.53,"You still don't win."],[1627.63,"Even though you killed my body."],[1630.59,"He who did not spare his own son, but delivered him up for us all, how shall he not with him also freely give us all things?"],[1638.29,"So this is a pretty clear restatement of what he was saying in those verses."],[1641.81,"If God was willing to offer his own son, if Christ was willing to die on the cross for us, how will he not also bring this through to completion?"],[1649.01,"Why would he do that"],[1654.65,"Why would he do all this and then not fulfill the rest of his promises?"],[1659.37,"Who shall bring a charge against God's elect?"],[1662.49,"It is God who justifies, who is he who condemns?"],[1666.33,"It is Christ who died, and furthermore is also risen, who is even at the right hand of God, who also makes intercession for us."],[1675.21,"So the word elect, I know, now carries Calvinist connotations."],[1682.41,"Okay, Calvinist connotations."],[1684.57,"Okay. But how is it used in Scripture?"],[1690.73,"So when St."],[1691.61,"Paul in Colossians refers to the elect angels angels, is he talking about the that were predestined for salvation in Christ?"],[1694.09,"No No, he's talking about the ones who are faithful, the ones who didn't rebel."],[1703.97,"And that's the way the word is used."],[1705.57,"The phrase elect angels occurs fairly frequently in Second Temple Jewish literature."],[1709.65,"And so elect is a word, it's a word that means really means called."],[1717.62,"Or it can mean, it can mean chosen, it can mean preferred."],[1723.06,"Or his beloved."],[1725.7,"That is used for angels."],[1727.62,"It's used for faithful people."],[1730.5,"It's used for Israel a lot in the Greek of the Old Testament."],[1736.42,"And when it's used to describe Israel it doesn't mean every single person who's a member of the nation of Israel is quote unquote saved scriptures are very clear that that's not true right some of them thought that though didn't they that's well later on but but yeah that's that's clearly not what it means like in the case of Israel right is that these are people chosen it means the faithful ones right and so he's uh the prophets move to talking about an elect remnant of Israel."],[1771.71,"That there's this peace."],[1772.83,"And that peace isn't that, well, God just chose a few to save who are just as wicked as all the other ones."],[1778.67,"It's that there's one remnant, one little remnant that remains faithful."],[1782.69,"This is the remnant that."],[1784.13,"This is in the context of the captivity in Babylon."],[1787.49,"And the exile."],[1789.41,"Where the idea that a remnant might survive is kind of starving."],[1793.67,"Well, even the ones who made it to captivity in Babylon are a tiny piece."],[1798.47,"Remember, ten tribes, really ten and a half, were wiped off the face of the earth."],[1805.03,"Most of Israel just ceased to exist."],[1807.75,"The Assyrians destroyed them."],[1809.83,"So you've got this remnant of the tribe of Judah and a little bit of Benjamin that even went into exile."],[1817.41,"Let alone were delivered and came back from the exile."],[1821.89,"Yeah, it's this it's this small remnant, but it's this remnant that's faithful."],[1825.35,"This is the remnant that uh God talks to Elijah about."],[1830.36,"What Elijah despairs, and he says, Look, I'm the last one left who's still faithful to you."],[1835.24,"He says, No, I have 10,000 who have not bowed their knee to Baal."],[1840.5,"That there is this elect remnant."],[1843.08,"And that means that those are the ones who are faithful, just like it."],[1847.0,"Those are the angels who are faithful."],[1848.84,"So it's saying, who is going to bring a charge against God's faithful people?"],[1854.86,"Who's going to come and accuse them of sin?"],[1856.78,"Who's going to come and say they need to be condemned because of their sins?"],[1862.14,"And this is appealing to a very common motif in Jewish thought."],[1868.54,"Uh, in second Temple Judaism, Saint Michael, the archangel, was the angel who is, he's referred to in Daniel as the prince of Judah."],[1882.44,"Of the Israelite people."],[1883.96,"He's sort of the angel that's assigned to them."],[1886.04,"And he talks about the prince of Persia, who's a demon, who the Persians worship, and the Prince of Greece, referring to Alexander's gods, who are coming."],[1896.69,"But Saint Michael is the sort of the guardian angel or the patron of Judah."],[1902.77,"So the idea of a nation having a patron, a heavenly patron, is not new in Orthodox Christianity."],[1909.57,"It goes all the way back into the Old Testament."],[1911.73,"It's just now there are humans who who have become sons of God and fill that role, not just angelic beings."],[1913.81,"But so St"],[1920.81,"Michael would sort of intercede for the nation."],[1925.91,"As Satan would come and accuse."],[1928.95,"And you see Satan doing that job in, for example, the book of Job."],[1934.13,"All the sons of God, meaning the angels, come to stand before God, and the Satan comes too."],[1941.81,"And he starts accusing Job."],[1944.23,"Oh, yeah, Job loves you and is faithful to you, but that's because you've made him rich and you've given him all these things."],[1949.99,"If you hadn't given him all those things, he wouldn't be faithful."],[1952.71,"You take those away, anyway."],[1954.05,"He's making these accusations."],[1957.13,"And so there was this parody between the Satan, who is seen as an angelic being, sometimes called Satanael and other names, and then Saint Michael on the other side."],[1968.81,"And this is where uh in Jude, which we'll get to eventually, uh he refers to uh when uh the archangel Michael and Satan contested over the body of Moses."],[1981.86,"This is a reference to the same tradition."],[1984.18,"Moses dies, and Satan comes and wants to claim him for death and corruption."],[1991.88,"And starts accusing him of his sins, and Saint Michael comes and says no and testifies in the other direction and intercedes, on his behalf."],[2002.66,"And this is what's being referred to when Christ during his ministry talks about, he says, when he sends out the 70, and they go and they cast out demons."],[2013.46,"He says, I saw Satan fall from heaven, like then."],[2020.16,"That's what it's referring to."],[2021.52,"It's referring to him being now thrown down."],[2024.42,"Because in the face of the gospel, he can't make accusations anymore."],[2028.26,"So this is what Saint Paul is referring to here."],[2031.3,"Who can come and make an accusation against?"],[2033.94,"He says, Because it's not now Saint Michael who's interceding."],[2037.62,"Now it's Jesus Christ who's interceding."],[2040.98,"And he's the one who died for us and rose again."],[2044.18,"He's the one who's justified us, who's cleansed us of our sins"],[2044.42,"He's the one who's saving us now who intercedes."],[2052.54,"So, and he's the judge, by the way."],[2055.42,"So, who is it who now can come and make it?"],[2058.14,"No one. No one."],[2061.11,"So he's already said, first Saint Paul says, there's no one on earth who can do anything to us."],[2068.17,"To combat what God is doing in our lives."],[2071.05,"You know, kill our body, we still win."],[2075.07,"Now we're a martyr."],[2076.67,"Uh, we just saying about the higher martyr of Michael."],[2079.85,"Now we're glorified."],[2081.61,"Um, now he's saying there's no spiritual power out there, the devil or any any of his demons who can, come and make an accusation at us because of who Christ is and what he's done for us"],[2096.29,"So we have nothing to fear from anybody."],[2100.05,"And so he summarizes this by saying, Who shall separate us from the love of Christ?"],[2104.53,"Shall tribulation or distress or persecution or famine or nakedness or peril or sword?"],[2109.97,"These are all the things, all the suffering, you know, all the bad things that can happen to us in this life."],[2116.29,"As it is written, this is from Psalm 44."],[2120.09,"For your sake we are killed all day long."],[2122.29,"We are accounted as sheep for the slaughter."],[2126.13,"Yet in all these things we are more than conquerors through him who loved us."],[2130.85,"So through Christ, who loves us. us, and showed that by dying for and rising again for our justification, as St"],[2139.69,"Paul has said."],[2144.73,"Meaning, what it actually says is sort of super conquerors, hyper, hyper-conquerors."],[2151.27,"This is that language that Christ himself used, like in St."],[2154.39,"John's Gospel. You know, this world you have troubles, but fear not, for I have overcome the world."],[2159.11,"And that he uses in the letters to the churches in the book of Revelation."],[2162.56,"To the one who overcomes."],[2164.66,"That overcome is the word conquer."],[2167.32,"So he said, We've super overcome all these things."],[2170.44,"All of these powers opposed us, both the earthly powers and the heavenly powers, the devil himself who. comes to accuse us, we've defeated them all in Christ"],[2181.04,"And so his final summary, verse 38 For I am persuaded that neither death nor life, nor angels, nor principalities, nor powers, nor things present, nor things to come, nor height, nor depth, nor any other created thing shall be able to separate us from the love of God, which is in Christ Jesus our Lord."],[2198.96,"So things on earth, things in heaven."],[2201.06,"Angels, demons, powers, and principalities in heavenly places, death, life, the sword, famine, anything that happens in this life can't separate us from the love of Christ."],[2211.14,"Because the work he's begun, he's going to bring to completion."],[2216.26,"What about other people?"],[2218.18,"What other people?"],[2220.34,"The uh the you know, the Calvinist emphasis is just as much on those who are condemned as those who are are saved."],[2229.79,"Um all of these things apply to faithful Christians."],[2239.59,"Um but the sheep and goats parable seems to indicate that it's what you do more than I don't want to say more than faith, but help me out."],[2255.05,"Well, you notice the one thing that he doesn't say is that you can't separate yourself."],[2263.81,"He says nothing can separate you."],[2266.45,"He doesn't say you can't separate yourself."],[2269.17,"He doesn't say you can't reject the love of Christ."],[2273.41,"He says if you accept it, no one can come in between that or break that."],[2279.94,"But he doesn't say you can't reject it yourself."],[2286.2,"Because he then he'd be contradicting himself, because he's already talked about people following after the flesh and it bringing about destruction."],[2293.77,"So these are words of assurance directed to the faithful in the midst of trials and tribulations and troubles, guilt which condemns us."],[2304.34,"In the face of all those things."],[2306.4,"Reminding us of the love of God, the work he's done for us, and the work that he'll bring to completion."],[2312.37,"But he doesn't say you don't participate in that."],[2322.93,"If you don't want it."],[2324.23,"God's still going to right."],[2325.91,"None of that."],[2327.27,"But the the foreknowledge part."],[2331.83,"Um if if you've been a faithful Christian and then you turn your back on it."],[2337.81,"God knew that."],[2339.49,"Yeah. So the God knows that too."],[2345.49,"Yeah. Yeah. Yeah."],[2348.21,"Yeah. Paul's saying, he who endures to the end will be saved"],[2349.85,"And so we get, you know, St."],[2355.61,"So that's the if we are following the Spirit on this road that leads to life."],[2363.81,"God will bring that to fruition."],[2368.45,"Who is dwelling within us."],[2369.65,"Enabling us to not give up and say separate ourselves."],[2377.07,"So this is like this is like the antique Elvin interpretation."],[2381.95,"Because it's it's not because God does everything to bring us into his love."],[2386.91,"Everybody. He does everything to bring us into his love."],[2390.03,"And it's within us that we choose to and to love him and to enter into that love or not to."],[2396.45,"So it's in it's in our choice to be able to do that."],[2398.21,"To walk according to the flesh or walk according to the side in one."],[2401.89,"To walk according to the flesh or walk according to the spirit."],[2404.53,"You're right, it's a it's definitely a reversal."],[2410.61,"Yeah. Yeah, but I think that reversal comes again from just taking it into context of what he's saying earlier in chapter eight."],[2417.65,"If you start with verse twenty-eight, you can take it in one direction."],[2422.13,"But if you take it in the context."],[2424.21,"Italicized words, the words that are added, they make a difference too."],[2431.57,"Because they're interpreting it according to a certain reading."],[2435.73,"Yeah. Yeah. And the use of the word predestined instead of foreordained makes a difference."],[2446.41,"Yeah, at least in the connotation."],[2448.65,"Yeah, at least in our ears."],[2452.43,"Because predestined for us brings them on this whole Calvinist idea of predestination."],[2458.27,"Which isn't really contained within the Greek word."],[2463.69,"Because remember the Greek word is God actually is him actively doing something."],[2469.85,"He's setting things in order."],[2472.02,"It's not an act of choosing."],[2473.54,"It's not an act of will."],[2475.92,"It's him actively doing something."],[2478.08,"It's taking what he has and ordering it."],[2481.65,"Yeah. In accordance."],[2483.49,"What he's created and setting these things in order."],[2486.85,"So it includes crossing the Red Sea and everything else."],[2491.49,"The giving of the law."],[2492.61,"Yeah, and this is recounted in in one of the prayers of St."],[2496.29,"Basil's liturgy. When we had fallen away, actually I think it's in St."],[2501.03,"John Chrysostom's liturgy."],[2502.55,"When we had fallen away, he did not cease to do all things to raise us up and to make us heirs of the kingdom which is to come."],[2512.23,"You know, he sent us the St."],[2515.59,"Basil. He sent us the law to aid us."],[2518.95,"He sent prophets."],[2519.75,"He sent it lists all the things."],[2521.51,"Finally, he sent his, spoke to us through his son and and goes through the life of Christ everything Christ did."],[2526.43,"It recounts all of that."],[2528.13,"All of the things that God did once we had fallen away in order to bring us back."],[2534.1,"And that's what St."],[2534.9,"Paul is talking about here."],[2536.34,"There's a wonderful Jewish song, I guess it's a hymn, called Dainu, which is Hebrew for it would have been enough for us."],[2547.54,"What they do is they they verse specifies one of the actions of God, and then says, if that's all he'd done, it would have been enough for us."],[2559.54,"And then but he it kind of builds, he did that, and he also did this, and that would have been enough for us."],[2566.66,"And so he did this and he did this and he also did that and that would have been enough for us and it keeps going."],[2568.18,"It's a I really like it"],[2576.14,"Of course it doesn't go far enough but it stops too early."],[2581.1,"Yeah. I think we should continue it."],[2584.14,"Yeah. We need to try to continue."],[2586.94,"So yeah that's what St."],[2589.02,"Paul is doing."],[2589.66,"And I think if you read it in context, you see how all of these statements relate."],[2595.36,"You know, why is he talking about the fact that angels and demons and principalities can't separate well that's what he's already been talking about."],[2602.02,"How these are summaries."],[2604.5,"Yeah there's some verses here that are um very very com comffortortinging uh encouraging verses and uh there's nothing and encouraging about Calvinist uh interpretation these are you know these are verses I think of when I'm discouraged yeah and that's how they're intended that's how they're intended listen next time as Father Stephen DeYoung continues his study of the scriptures on the Whole Counsel of God father Stephen's email address is wholecounsel at ancient faith.com."],[2648.02,"That's wholecounsel at ancient faith.com."]],"o":[{"t":21.68,"lv":2,"h":"Recap: Romans 8-11 and the \"Romans' Road\"","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Picking up at <b>Romans 8:28</b>; need to catch up on first half of ch. 8</li><li class=\"d0\">Chs. <b>8-11</b>: passages <b>Calvinists get very excited about</b></li><li class=\"d1\">their interpretation = basis for \"their whole thing\"</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Romans' Road</b> in Calvinist circles</li><li class=\"d1\">hop-skip through Romans: <b>1-3</b>, first half of <b>5</b>, then <b>8-11</b></li><li class=\"d1\">read along a certain trajectory → \"you can kind of get Calvinism out of them\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Problem: parts <b>skipped and ignored</b> → passages taken <b>out of context</b></li><li class=\"d0\">So set 2nd half of ch. 8 in terms of the 1st half: the <b>lead-in</b>, themes, <b>flow of the argument</b></li></ul>"},{"t":127.14,"lv":3,"h":"Flesh vs body","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Ch. 8 opens: contrast of walking by the <b>flesh</b> vs by the <b>Spirit</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Flesh</b> and <b>body</b> - used interchangeably by us, but <b>different in Paul</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Body</b> = physical body → <b>redeemed</b> when Christ returns</li><li class=\"d1\">1 Corinthians 15: resurrection, transformation, transfiguration of bodies</li><li class=\"d1\">physicality, material creation = <b>not bad, not evil</b> - <b>contra Greek thought</b> of the time</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Flesh</b> = part of our current body <b>prone to sin</b>, \"mortal flesh\"</li><li class=\"d1\">subject to sin and death</li><li class=\"d1\">received after <b>expulsion from paradise</b>: \"garments of skin\"</li><li class=\"d1\">fathers: not a \"neat leather outfit\"; a <b>negative transformation</b> - mortal, therefore subject to sin and wandering</li><li class=\"d1\">parallel: Christ's body <b>transformed</b> at the resurrection</li></ul>"},{"t":250.07,"lv":3,"h":"Two ways to walk","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Two ways to live</li><li class=\"d1\">follow the <b>flesh</b>: gratify food, drink, sex, wealth</li><li class=\"d1\">follow the <b>Spirit</b>: Holy Spirit now <b>dwells within us</b> because Christ purified us; seek to gratify God</li><li class=\"d0\">Flesh path → <b>death</b> - chasing a world that is <b>passing away</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Spirit path → <b>life</b>: eternal life, life in the world to come</li></ul>"},{"t":315.55,"lv":2,"h":"Suffering, the Spirit as down payment, adoption","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Paul turns to <b>sufferings of this life</b></li><li class=\"d1\">Spirit within = <b>down payment, deposit</b> on the world to come; beginning of our salvation</li><li class=\"d1\">hope in the midst of troubles; <b>proof</b> of the glory that awaits</li><li class=\"d0\">Paul's own troubles: <b>shipwrecked, stoned several times, beaten</b> - not worth comparing to the glory</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Adoption</b> not just a metaphor of love</li><li class=\"d1\">God's family = <b>royal family</b></li><li class=\"d1\">OT <b>sons of God</b> = highest rank of angels, God's council</li><li class=\"d1\">adoption = spiritual riches + role in world to come <b>helping God govern creation</b>, sharing Christ's rule</li><li class=\"d0\">If we doubt in suffering → Spirit is the <b>signifier and down payment</b> that it is real</li><li class=\"d0\">Context for <b>8:28</b> - so we don't misunderstand when he talks <b>predestination</b></li></ul>"},{"t":470.33,"lv":2,"h":"Q&amp;A: garments of skin","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Q: Genesis - clothed in skin; Hebrew vs Greek differences?</li><li class=\"d0\">Basically says <b>garments of skin</b> in both</li><li class=\"d0\">Other reading because it follows God <b>killing animals</b> → \"God made them clothes\"</li><li class=\"d1\">\"doesn't make a lot of sense in context\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Fathers: God killed animals to <b>show them what death was</b></li><li class=\"d1\">told they'd die; now shown</li><li class=\"d1\">then clothed with a body <b>like the animals'</b> - mortal, subject to death</li><li class=\"d0\">Animals were <b>mortal before the fall</b> too</li><li class=\"d1\">for humans immortality would have come by eating from the <b>tree of life</b></li></ul>"},{"t":563.22,"lv":2,"h":"Romans 8:28-30 - The passage read","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>Verse 28</b>: all things work together for good to those who love God, <b>called according to his purpose</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Verses 29-30</b>: whom he <b>foreknew</b> he also <b>predestined</b> to be conformed to the image of his Son, firstborn among many brethren</li><li class=\"d1\">whom he predestined, he also <b>called</b>; called → <b>justified</b>; justified → <b>glorified</b></li><li class=\"d0\">\"I can get Calvinism out of that\" - <b>especially out of context</b></li></ul>"},{"t":609.04,"lv":2,"h":"8:28 - all things work together","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">First verse mostly <b>non-controversial</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Context: just talked about <b>suffering</b></li><li class=\"d1\">even painful, unpleasant things God uses <b>for our good</b></li><li class=\"d1\">not only endure them: through them <b>mature spiritually</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Orthodox Study Bible</b>: words in <b>italics</b> aren't in the original - added by interpreter, be careful</li><li class=\"d0\">\"According to <b>his</b> purpose\" - <b>his</b> is not there</li><li class=\"d1\">literally: called according to <b>a purpose</b></li><li class=\"d1\">\"his purpose\" = God chose some for his reasons; \"<b>a purpose</b>\" = called <b>to do something</b></li><li class=\"d1\">so not necessarily about salvation</li><li class=\"d0\">\"To be\" conformed in v.29 also added</li></ul>"},{"t":749.7,"lv":2,"h":"The Calvinist reading and its problem","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Calvinist view: <i>Ordo Salutis</i> = <b>order of salvation</b>, steps in sequence</li><li class=\"d1\">foreknows → predestines → calls → justifies → glorifies</li><li class=\"d0\">Problem: all verbs in the <b>same past tense</b> - nothing indicates <b>order</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Paul's point: God doesn't just do <b>one</b> of these</li><li class=\"d1\">not \"stage two\" - <b>all or nothing</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Church fathers (pre-Calvin, most hadn't read <b>Augustine</b>): their problem = Christ's \"<b>many are called, but few are chosen</b>\"</li><li class=\"d1\">here: everyone predestined is called, everyone called is justified</li><li class=\"d0\">So dig into the words</li></ul>"},{"t":847.7,"lv":2,"h":"\"Foreknew\" = knew","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Doesn't say he knew <b>something about</b> them - just that he <b>knew them</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Sheep and goats</b>: \"be gone from me, <b>I never knew you</b>\"</li><li class=\"d1\">not literal - Christ created everyone</li><li class=\"d1\">basis: he was hungry and <b>they hadn't fed him</b>; same in other parables</li><li class=\"d0\">Knowledge refers to <b>faithful and unfaithful</b> alike</li><li class=\"d0\">Related OT language: names written in the <b>book of life</b></li><li class=\"d1\">common in OT, ancient Near Eastern, <b>Second Temple</b> literature</li><li class=\"d1\">heavenly books record everything; opened at the last judgment → God <b>knows about it</b>, nothing slips past</li><li class=\"d0\">Group = <b>the faithful</b>, who God has a purpose for</li><li class=\"d0\"><i>proginōskō</i> = <i>pro</i> (before) + <i>ginōskō</i> (know) → \"knew beforehand\"</li><li class=\"d1\">Calvinists: knew before creation</li></ul>"},{"t":1005.53,"lv":3,"h":"\"Predestined\" = set in order beforehand","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><i>proorizō</i> - set up to be <b>conformed to the likeness of his Son</b>, firstborn among many brethren</li><li class=\"d1\">= ch. 8 language: sons of God, heirs, <b>royal family</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><i>horizō</i> = <b>set in order</b></li><li class=\"d1\">used of a general <b>setting troops in order</b> for battle: organize, put in place</li><li class=\"d0\">God, <b>outside time</b>, not surprised, <b>knew who his faithful would be</b></li><li class=\"d1\">not general \"there will be some faithful people\"</li><li class=\"d0\">So in advance he <b>set things in place</b> for them to reach the destiny</li><li class=\"d1\">Christ incarnate, crucified, risen on 3rd day, ascended, <b>Spirit poured out</b></li><li class=\"d1\">all happened long before we existed - but he knew us</li><li class=\"d1\">so I can say Christ died for <b>my</b> sins, rose for <b>my</b> salvation</li><li class=\"d0\">God at work since <b>expulsion from paradise</b>: the law, OT, NT, my own life</li><li class=\"d1\">under <b>no obligation</b></li></ul>"},{"t":1194.91,"lv":3,"h":"Foreordained, not fatalistic","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">What's predestined = the <b>whole plan of salvation</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Foreordained</b> better than predestined</li><li class=\"d1\">\"predestined\" sounds <b>fatalistic</b> - born with a destiny, nothing to do</li><li class=\"d1\">original: beforehand he <b>ordered</b> things so we could become like Christ, <b>fellow heirs</b></li></ul>"},{"t":1234.15,"lv":2,"h":"Firstborn, inheritance and \"sons\"","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Reiterate: <b>inheritance</b> in this culture</li><li class=\"d1\">firstborn inherited everything - but must <b>distribute</b> to other heirs</li><li class=\"d1\">man asks Christ to tell brother to divide the inheritance → \"come follow me\"</li><li class=\"d1\">not one person gets it all, others \"lump it\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Matters for <b>Jacob and Esau</b> in ch. 9</li><li class=\"d0\">Christ = firstborn, inherits <b>whole creation</b>, gives <b>a share to us</b> as adopted brothers</li><li class=\"d0\">Why <b>brothers / sons</b>: modern translations mute it (\"very PC\")</li><li class=\"d1\">a <b>son is an heir</b> in this culture</li><li class=\"d1\">Paul's point: <b>women who believe become sons</b>, heirs</li><li class=\"d1\">may be 2nd-4th class on earth in 1st c. - in God's royal family, <b>not lesser</b></li></ul>"},{"t":1361.21,"lv":2,"h":"Called, justified, glorified","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>Called</b> = we <b>heard the gospel</b>, it was proclaimed</li><li class=\"d1\">if Christ did it all but we never heard → wouldn't avail</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Justified</b> (ch. 3) = made righteous, share God's righteousness</li><li class=\"d1\">at judgment we're the <b>sheep, not the goats</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Glorified</b>: all <b>past tense</b> - many saints already by Paul's time</li><li class=\"d1\">\"look at <b>St. Elias</b>, the OT prophets\"</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>retroactive</b> for OT saints, glorified with us</li><li class=\"d1\">part of the <b>harrowing of hell</b> depiction</li></ul>"},{"t":1444.06,"lv":3,"h":"Why Paul says this: hope","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Context again = <b>why we should have hope</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Imagine: followed Christ, family disowned you, outcast in city, <b>lost livelihood</b></li><li class=\"d1\">tempted: \"maybe we're wrong - God isn't taking care of me\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Paul: <b>long before you were conceived</b>, God knew you</li><li class=\"d1\">OT, incarnation, ministry, death, resurrection, ascension, Spirit</li><li class=\"d1\">provided for you to hear the gospel; cleansed you</li><li class=\"d0\">\"Do you really think he's <b>not going to bring this through to the end</b>?\"</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Not</b> about whether our actions are involved - he already settled that: <b>flesh perishes, Spirit lives</b></li><li class=\"d1\">he's addressing people prone to <b>despair</b>; a powerful reason not to</li><li class=\"d0\">Comment: Calvinism often <b>discouraged</b> people - wickedness, inability, \"hope God picked you\"; already decided</li></ul>"},{"t":1592.48,"lv":2,"h":"Romans 8:31-34 - If God is for us","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>Verse 31</b>: if God is for us, <b>who can be against us</b>?</li><li class=\"d1\">again in the context of <b>suffering</b></li><li class=\"d1\">who can stop him bringing it to completion?</li><li class=\"d1\">if they kill you → eternal life: \"you still don't win\"</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Verse 32</b>: he who did not spare his own Son - <b>how shall he not freely give us all things</b>?</li><li class=\"d1\">restates 29-30: if willing to offer his Son, he'll complete the promises</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Verses 33-34</b>: who brings a charge against <b>God's elect</b>? God justifies; Christ died, is risen, at the right hand, <b>intercedes</b></li></ul>"},{"t":1675.21,"lv":2,"h":"\"Elect\" in Scripture","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>Elect</b> now carries Calvinist connotations - but how is it used?</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Colossians</b>: \"elect angels\" = the ones who <b>didn't rebel</b>, not predestined for salvation</li><li class=\"d1\">phrase common in <b>Second Temple</b> literature</li><li class=\"d0\">Elect = <b>called, chosen, preferred, beloved</b></li><li class=\"d1\">used of angels, faithful people, <b>Israel</b> in OT Greek</li><li class=\"d1\">of Israel: <b>not</b> every member \"saved\" - Scripture clear</li><li class=\"d0\">Prophets: <b>elect remnant</b> = the <b>faithful</b>, not \"a few just as wicked as the rest\"</li><li class=\"d1\">context: <b>Babylonian exile</b></li><li class=\"d1\"><b>ten and a half tribes</b> wiped out by Assyrians; Judah and a bit of Benjamin went into exile</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Elijah</b>: \"I'm the last faithful\" → \"I have <b>10,000</b> who have not bowed the knee to Baal\"</li><li class=\"d0\">\"Who will accuse God's <b>faithful</b>?\"</li></ul>"},{"t":1862.14,"lv":3,"h":"Michael, Satan and the accuser","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Common motif in <b>Second Temple Judaism</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Michael</b> = \"prince of Judah\" in Daniel; angel assigned to Israel</li><li class=\"d1\">also prince of <b>Persia</b> (demon the Persians worship), prince of <b>Greece</b></li><li class=\"d1\">nation having a <b>heavenly patron</b> goes back to OT - now humans who are sons of God fill the role too</li><li class=\"d0\">Michael <b>intercedes</b>; <b>Satan accuses</b></li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Job</b>: sons of God stand before God, the Satan accuses - \"he's only faithful because you made him rich\"</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Jude</b>: Michael and Satan contest over <b>body of Moses</b> - same tradition</li><li class=\"d1\">Satan claims him for death, accuses; Michael testifies the other way</li><li class=\"d0\">Christ sends out the <b>70</b> - \"I saw <b>Satan fall</b> from heaven\"</li><li class=\"d1\">thrown down: in face of the gospel <b>can't accuse</b> anymore</li><li class=\"d0\">Now <b>Jesus Christ intercedes</b> - died, rose, justified us, and he's the <b>judge</b></li><li class=\"d0\">No one on earth can stop it (kill us → <b>martyr, glorified</b>); no spiritual power can accuse → \"nothing to fear\"</li></ul>"},{"t":2100.05,"lv":2,"h":"Romans 8:35-39 - Nothing can separate us","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>Verse 35</b>: who shall separate us from the <b>love of Christ</b>? tribulation, distress, persecution, famine, nakedness, peril, sword</li><li class=\"d1\">= all the bad things in this life</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Psalm 44</b>: \"for your sake we are killed all day long, sheep for the slaughter\"</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Verse 37</b>: <b>more than conquerors</b> through him who loved us</li><li class=\"d1\">literally <b>\"super conquerors,\" hyper-conquerors</b></li><li class=\"d1\">Christ: \"in this world you have troubles, but <b>I have overcome the world</b>\" (John)</li><li class=\"d1\">Revelation, letters to the churches: \"to the one who <b>overcomes</b>\" = conquers</li><li class=\"d1\">defeated earthly and heavenly powers, the devil who accuses</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Verses 38-39</b>: neither death nor life, angels, principalities, powers, things present or to come, height, depth, any created thing</li><li class=\"d1\">earth and heaven, angels, demons, sword, famine → can't separate us</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>work he began, he will bring to completion</b></li></ul>"},{"t":2216.26,"lv":2,"h":"Q&amp;A: others, and can we reject it?","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Q: what about <b>other people</b>? Calvinist emphasis equally on the <b>condemned</b></li><li class=\"d0\">All of this applies to <b>faithful Christians</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Q: sheep and goats seems to be what you <b>do</b>, more than faith</li><li class=\"d0\">Notice what Paul <b>doesn't</b> say: that you can't separate <b>yourself</b></li><li class=\"d1\">\"nothing can separate you\" - but doesn't say you can't <b>reject the love of Christ</b></li><li class=\"d1\">if you accept it, no one can come between</li><li class=\"d1\">otherwise he'd <b>contradict himself</b> - already said following the flesh → destruction</li><li class=\"d0\">Words of <b>assurance</b> to the faithful amid trials, tribulations, <b>guilt which condemns us</b></li><li class=\"d1\">reminds of God's love, work done, work he'll complete</li><li class=\"d1\">but doesn't say you don't participate</li><li class=\"d0\">Comment: if a faithful Christian turns away, God <b>knew that</b> too</li><li class=\"d1\">Paul: \"<b>he who endures to the end will be saved</b>\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Following the Spirit on the road to life → God brings it to <b>fruition</b>, Spirit within enabling us not to give up</li></ul>"},{"t":2377.07,"lv":3,"h":"Q&amp;A: the reversal of Calvinism","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Comment: an <b>anti-Calvinist</b> reading - God does everything to bring us into his love; <b>our choice</b> to enter it or not</li><li class=\"d1\">walk by flesh or by Spirit</li><li class=\"d0\">Fr S: reversal comes from reading in <b>context of earlier ch. 8</b></li><li class=\"d1\">start at v.28 and you can go one direction</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Italicized words</b> make a difference - interpret a certain way</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Predestined</b> vs <b>foreordained</b></li><li class=\"d1\">predestined brings the whole Calvinist idea - <b>not contained in the Greek word</b></li><li class=\"d1\">Greek: God <b>actively doing something</b>, setting things in order</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>not an act of choosing, not an act of will</b></li><li class=\"d1\">takes what he has created and orders it</li><li class=\"d1\">includes <b>crossing the Red Sea</b>, giving of the Law</li></ul>"},{"t":2492.61,"lv":3,"h":"Liturgy prayer and Dayenu","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Recounted in a prayer of the liturgy (<b>St. Basil</b>'s - \"actually I think <b>Chrysostom's</b>\")</li><li class=\"d1\">\"when we had fallen away, he did not cease to do all things to raise us up and make us <b>heirs of the kingdom</b>\"</li><li class=\"d1\">sent the law, prophets, finally spoke through his Son; recounts the life of Christ</li><li class=\"d1\">= what Paul is talking about</li><li class=\"d0\">Jewish hymn <b>Dayenu</b> = \"it would have been enough for us\"</li><li class=\"d1\">each verse names an action of God, \"if that's all he'd done...\"; it builds</li><li class=\"d1\">\"doesn't go far enough, stops too early\" - \"we need to continue it\"</li></ul>"},{"t":2589.02,"lv":2,"h":"Reading it in context: comfort","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Read in context, all statements <b>relate</b></li><li class=\"d1\">why angels, demons, principalities can't separate us - that's what he's been talking about; <b>summaries</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Very <b>comforting, encouraging</b> verses</li><li class=\"d1\">\"nothing encouraging about the Calvinist interpretation\"</li><li class=\"d1\">Comment: verses I think of when discouraged</li><li class=\"d0\">\"That's how they're <b>intended</b>\"</li></ul>"}]});
